{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "am-03-profit-adulteration-free-water",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Profit, Adulteration, and Free Water",
    "description": "Separate selling price, mixture cost, and ingredient cost to solve adulteration and profit-linked mixture questions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with the familiar idea that water costs nothing in a milk mixture. Show that profit is measured against the mixture's actual cost, not the pure milk cost. Recover the mixture cost from selling price, use alligation with zero-cost water, convert ratios to percentages, and then generalize to two priced ingredients and weighted profit rates.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Teach why free water behaves like an ingredient with cost zero. When a mixture is sold at a stated selling price and profit, first recover the mixture's cost price, then use alligation with the ingredient costs. Cover selling an adulterated milk mixture at the original milk cost and deriving the water : milk ratio, converting that ratio to water percentage, and mixing two priced ingredients for a target profit. Include weighted-profit questions where parts of one stock are sold at different profit rates to give a stated overall profit. Emphasise the distinction between the cost of pure milk, the cost of the mixture, and the selling price; many wrong answers come from mixing those three layers."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's see why free water can create profit.\n\nPure milk has a cost. Water has cost zero. If a seller mixes the two but sells the mixture at the old milk price, the selling price has not risen — the mixture's cost has fallen.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose pure milk costs ₹60 per litre. A seller mixes in free water but still sells the mixture at ₹60 per litre.\n\nIf the mixture actually costs ₹50 per litre, the seller earns ₹10 on ₹50 — a 20% profit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The three prices are different ideas:\n\npure milk cost = ₹60\nmixture cost = ₹50\nselling price = ₹60\n\nProfit is based on mixture cost. Keeping those layers separate is the key to adulteration questions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
